{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second law of thermodynamics tells us\nthat everything in the universe tends towards"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4480,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disorder. And in complex systems, chaos is\nthe norm. So you'd naturally expect the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be messy. And yet, we can observe occasions\nof spontaneous order, the synchronization"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18240,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of metronomes, the perfectly timed orbits\nof moons, the simultaneous flashes of fireflies,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even the regular beating of your heart.\nWhat puts these things in order in spite of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31040,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nature's tendency for disorder."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39252,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "London was opened to much excitement. But\nas crowds filled the bridge, it began to wobble"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46612,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back and forth. Police started restricting\naccess to the bridge, but that only resulted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52052,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in long lines to get on the wobble was unaffected.\nTwo days later, the bridge which had cost"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58852,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "18 million pounds, was fully closed, and it\nwouldn't reopen for another two years. So"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66532,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what went wrong?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68772,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, it's long been known that armies should\nbreak step when crossing bridges. This dates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73732,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back to an accident in 1831. When 74 men from\nthe 60 of Rifle Corps were marching across"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79892,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Broughton suspension bridge in northern\nEngland. It collapsed under their synchronized"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84772,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "footsteps. 60 men fell into the river 20 of\nwhom suffered injuries like broken bones or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90772,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "concussions. Luckily, no one was killed. But\nafter this, the British Army ordered all troops"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97572,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to break step when crossing bridges."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100932,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now look at the people walking across the\nMillennium Bridge. Most of them are walking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106692,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in step with each other. But they are not\npart of an army. They're random members of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113092,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the public so why are they walking together?\nAnd why couldn't a modern bridge designed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119012,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for heavy pedestrian traffic handle this?\nWell, to understand it, we have to go back"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125012,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "350 years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128932,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1656, famous Dutch physicist Christian\nHuygens created the first working pendulum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135012,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clock. The goal was to help sailors figure\nout where they were on the globe. Latitude"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141092,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be judged by measuring the position of\nthe sun or stars. But for longitude you also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146532,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to know the time at some fixed location,\nsay your home port. But clocks at the time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152052,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were routinely out by around 15 minutes a\nday. So they were effectively useless. wagons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158052,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pendulum clocks by contrast, were accurate\nto around 10 to 15 seconds per day. Huygens plan"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164932,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was to attach his clocks to a heavy hanging\nmass on the ship, so they wouldn't get tossed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169812,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around by the rolling seas. His plan called\nfor two clocks in case one stopped or was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175812,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "damaged."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177572,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But testing out this arrangement while at\nhome sick in February 1665. He made a remarkable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183812,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discovery. To have his clocks hung from a\nwood beam across some chairs. Watching the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190212,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pendulums sway back and forth for hours. He\nnoticed after half an hour or so they would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196212,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spontaneously synchronize. As one clock swung\none way, the second would swing the other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201812,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way. As one would tick, the other would talk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206452,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So he tried disturbing the clocks. He set\nthem ticking out of sync but again, within"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211652,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "30 minutes or so they were back to the same\nlockstep. Wiggins thought this strange sympathy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217572,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of clocks must have been caused by air currents\nbetween the pendulums, so he placed a large"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222772,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "board in between them, but their clocks continued\nto sync up. It wasn't the air currents."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229892,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When he separated the clocks, the synchrony\nwould disappear, their times drifting apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234932,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But when he brought them back together, the\nsynchrony returned. Wiggins realized the two"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240132,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clocks were synchronizing because they were\nhung from the same wood beam. He transferred"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245412,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanical vibrations from one clock to the\nother, making the two oscillators coupled."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251572,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wegens was the first to observe this kind\nof spontaneous synchronization in inanimate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256612,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects. And although he qualitatively described\nwhat was happening, he was only a few decades"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261892,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ago that scientists started fleshing out a\nrigorous theory of synchronization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270292,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You may have seen this demo where you put\nseveral metronomes on a light wobbly platform"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275332,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and start them out of sync."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282772,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's trickier than people make it look."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286612,
    "dDurationMs": 16800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you do get it to work, though. It's kind\nof magical."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305972,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These metronomes don't have exactly the same\nnatural frequency, and yet they still beat"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311172,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in time. To understand how this works, it's\neasiest to first consider a couple metronomes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316852,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oscillating in sync with each other. When\nthe large masses accelerate to the left, they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321812,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "push the platform to the right. And when they\naccelerate to the right, they push the platform"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326052,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the left. So the center of mass of the\nsystem always stays roughly in the same spot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331812,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, if you start another metronome completely\nout of sync with the first two, the motion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336372,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the platform gives it a kick every half\nswing, speeding it up until it's in time with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341572,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first two."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359092,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This works, regardless of \nthe number of metronomes, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361652,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you have, the platform just goes whichever"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363972,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way the majority of metronomes are pushing\nit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368452,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can represent the position of a metronome\npendulum or any other oscillator as a point"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373172,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a circle. This shows its phase that is\nwhat part of the cycle it's in. So you could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379092,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call the rightmost point of the pendulum zero\ndegrees. And then the leftmost point is 180"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384372,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "degrees. And as the pendulum oscillates back\nand forth, the point goes around the circle,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390212,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the higher the frequency of the oscillator,\nthe faster that point goes around."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396132,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So this represents two metronomes with different\nfrequencies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401092,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this represents two metronomes with the\nsame frequency, but completely out of phase."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406772,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the metronomes are synchronized in phase,\ntheir dots go around the circle together,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412452,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can use this depiction to illustrate a\nmathematical model for the synchronizing behavior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417092,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we've been looking at. It's called the Kuramoto\nmodel. It says the rate each dot goes around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423332,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the circle equals its natural frequency, plus\nsome amount related to how far it is from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430132,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all the other dots. And the size of this term\nis determined by the coupling strength. I"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435412,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like to think of it actually visually by thinking\nabout people that are running around a track,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440292,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like suppose you're running with your friend,\nand maybe your friend is faster than you,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444692,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your friend says, you know, come on, move\nit or hurry it up. Because you're dawdling,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448692,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you're slow, you're falling behind. So if\nyou have enough fortitude, and you know, you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454772,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "try hard enough, and if the friend is sympathetic\nenough to slow down, then the coupling between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459492,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you is strong enough to overcome that inherent\ndifference in your natural running speeds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464212,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you're not very good friends, or, you\nknow, if you can't quite suck it up to move"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469332,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yourself faster, then the coupling will not\nbe strong enough to overcome that difference"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473892,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in one person will start lapping the other,\nthe fireflies of Southeast Asia are apparently"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479172,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good enough friends, because they synchronize\ntheir flashes. Even though each one has its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484532,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own particular frequency at which it likes\nto flash, they coupled to each other strongly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489252,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough so that hundreds, even 1000s can flash\ntogether in the same split second. There's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495492,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a great simulation of this by Nicky Case,\nyou start with individual fireflies just doing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500852,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their thing. And then you can turn on the\ninteraction between them. Now in the Kuramoto"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505652,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model, this would mean every Firefly has an\neffect on every other one. But in this simulation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511012,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a firefly is only affected by its neighbors.\nIf it sees a flash close by it nudges its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516852,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "internal clock forward a little bit, so it'll\nflash sooner than it would have otherwise."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521892,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, what's remarkable about this is even\nthough the interactions are small and close"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526692,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "range, over time, you can see waves traveling\nthrough all the fireflies, and eventually,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533572,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they're all flashing at once."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537492,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like you might think if you increase the coupling,\nyou just sort of gradually get a system more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541652,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and more synchronized. That's not what happens.\nIt's sort of like the way water doesn't gradually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546532,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "freeze as you lower the temperature, its water,\nwater water as you're lowering the temperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551012,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then at a critical temperature, the molecules\nsuddenly start to change their state and become"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556292,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solid instead of liquid. And this is a sort\nof time rather than space version of the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561892,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing. They sort of lock their phases in time,\nonce you pass a critical level of coupling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568532,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And at that point, the sort of crystallization "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571172,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in time is the phenomenon \nthat we call synchronization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576372,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is an audience in Budapest applauding\nafter a performance. But what happens next"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581412,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is completely spontaneous. They're not being\ninstructed by anyone to see if you can spot"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587812,
    "dDurationMs": 9600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the phase transition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602532,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this phenomenon of synchronization that we've\nbeen talking about one of the things that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605972,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "i find most appealing about it is how universal\nit is that it occurs at every scale of nature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612372,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from subatomic to cosmic \nit uses every communication "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617012,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "channel that nature has ever devised from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619732,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational interactions electrical interactions\nchemical mechanical i mean you name it anyway"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626052,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the two things can influence each other nature\nuses that to get things in sync"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632132,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take our own moon for example we only ever\nsee one side of it because it rotates on its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637332,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "axis exactly once for every time it goes around\nthe earth we say it is tidally locked to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643892,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earth and this is a common effect in our solar\nsystem there are 34 moons that are tidally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649812,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "locked to their planet the way this happens\ngoes something like this a moon starts out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655012,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with its own rotational frequency but the\ngravitational attraction to the planet is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659892,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stronger on the side closer to the planet\nand so it distorts the moon into an egg shape"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665172,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is greatly exaggerated here as the moon\ncontinues to orbit and rotate on its axis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671252,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those bulges swing out of alignment with the\nplanet and so the gravitational force on them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676052,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is constantly pulling them back into alignment\nand this slows the rotation of the moon until"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681652,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it is locked to the planet if the moon is\ninitially rotating too slowly this same mechanism"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687652,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can speed it up until it's locked there are\nall kinds of other beautiful synchronization"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693332,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phenomena in our solar system the three innermost\nmoons of jupiter io europa and ganymede are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700132,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not only tidally locked to the planet they're\nalso in a one to four orbital resonance with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705332,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other for every time ganymede goes around\njupiter europa goes around twice and io four"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711492,
    "dDurationMs": 320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716612,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the 1950s some russian chemists went looking\nfor a chemical reaction that would oscillate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722452,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like a chemical analog of a pendulum like\ncould you get something going back and forth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726932,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say between blue and orange over and over\nagain and naively you might say that's impossible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733972,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because there's principles of thermodynamics\nwhich say that closed systems just increase"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740532,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their entropy over time that they're just\ngoing to come to equilibrium but there's no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744452,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "principle in chemistry or thermodynamics that\nsays you have to go monotonically to equilibrium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750292,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are allowed to oscillate and damp out\nto equilibrium in an facilitatory way this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755812,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is exactly what Boris Belousov off and later\nAnatol Zhabotinsky discovered so this reaction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761572,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is known as the Belousov Zhabotinsky or BZ\nreaction i've sped it up because it can continue"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768212,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for half an hour or more oscillating between\nthese colors now it spends more time on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773652,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "burnt orange color so i sped up those sections\nmore it's very spectacular and it's kind of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779572,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shocking to see a chemical reaction doing\nthese periodic changes in color like chemicals"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784772,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acting like a clock like a pendulum so the\nstirred reaction has the advantage that you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790452,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you really get a sense of the collectivity\nof you know i don't know quadrillions of molecules"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795732,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "avogadro's number of molecules all doing the\nsame thing at the same time on the other hand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801092,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you don't stir if you just put like a petri\ndish of the bz reaction you can see something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807252,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even more amazing i think which is that you\ncan see spiral waves of color or target patterns"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812852,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanding circles of color moving through\nthe liquid maybe i should emphasize the liquid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819252,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself is not moving it's not like we're seeing\nripples on a pond but what's not still is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824932,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chemical concentrations you can see these\nblue waves in the bz reaction that are chemical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831972,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves not not water waves and that will just\npropagate and they move at a constant speed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837652,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and or they can look like a spiral that just\ngrows and grows and spins around and what's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842452,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really spooky and uncanny about this is that\nthe same phenomenon is seen in the heart you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847732,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can see spiral waves of electrical excitation\nin a heart that look exactly like the spiral"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853252,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves in chemical oscillations and chemical\nwaves in the bz reaction and this was the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858692,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sort of thing that inspired my mentor a guy\nnamed art winfrey who used chemical reaction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865332,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves to give himself insight into cardiac\narrhythmias you know you may have heard the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870532,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most deadly kind of a arrhythmia that the\nkind that will kill you it really in a matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873972,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of minutes ventricular arrhythmias ventricular\nfibrillation in particular winfrey's work"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880052,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seeing these rotating spirals on hearts as\nwell as in chemistry led him to a theory about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887252,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what's really causing ventricular fibrillation\nand how could we design for example better"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892372,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "defibrillators"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893892,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That are gentler, that could be a good outcome "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896452,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this theory. You know, \nthe lack of synchronization"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898852,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a fibrillation heart is what causes no\nblood to be pumped, and then sudden death"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902772,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ensues. So too little synchronization is obviously\na problem. But too much synchronization can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909892,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also cause trouble. Remember, \nthe wobbly Millennium "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913412,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bridge, it was all apparently down to something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916052,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called crowd synchrony Was it the people walking\nin step that caused it to oscillate? Actually,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922692,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kind of the opposite. The Millennium Bridge\nwas designed to look like a ribbon of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927812,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So its construction is unique. Unlike a typical\nsuspension bridge, its supporting cables run"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933492,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alongside it stretched taut, like guitar strings.\nIn the civil engineering literature, all designers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940932,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know that you do not build a footbridge with\na resonant frequency equal to the frequency"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945812,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of human walking. So we take about two strides\nper second one with your left foot one with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951332,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your right foot. So everybody who takes civil\nengineering knows if people are going to walk"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956612,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the bridge, It better not have a resonant\nfrequency in the vertical direction of two"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960372,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hertz. Okay, everybody knows that, including\nthe people who, who built the Millennium Bridge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966052,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what they didn't know. And what was new\nthat day is that half the frequency is also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970772,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "important, a frequency of one cycle a second,\nwhich is the frequency with which you put"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976932,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down, say, your left foot, half the time you're\ndoing your left foot. So why does that matter?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982612,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because when you're walking across a bridge,\nand you put your left foot down, you put a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985812,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tiny force sideways on the bridge. And normally,\nthat wouldn't matter because people are all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991252,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "walking at their own pace, \nthey're not synchronized, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994132,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so their sideways forces which are only about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996372,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a 10th, as big as their downward forces that "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998452,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they impart on the bridge, \nthat would be negligible,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001012,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it wouldn't do anything to the bridge.\nBut if the bridge happens to have a sideways"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005652,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frequency of one cycle a second, which the\nMillennium Bridge had happened did, then people"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011812,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can actually start to get the bridge moving\na little bit. After the bridge was closed,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017012,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "engineers got their colleagues to walk across\nit in increasing numbers, while they measured"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021252,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its acceleration. With 50 people on the bridge,\nthere was very little motion. At 100, the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028052,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vibrations had barely increased at 156, there\nwas still no wobble, but with just 10 more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036212,
    "dDurationMs": 9280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people 166 the acceleration grew dramatically.\nThe bridge swayed, just like it had on opening"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1045492,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "day, the system had undergone a phase transition.\nIf people can get the bridge moving a little."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052132,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out, people don't like to walk on\na platform that's moving a little bit sideways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056692,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you've ever been in a train, that's kind\nof going faster, if you stand up in a rowboat,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060852,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it starts moving sideways, people spread\ntheir legs apart, to try to stabilize themselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066052,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And they will actually start to walk in step.\nWith the sideways motion of the bridge, you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071412,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can see footage from the BBC, of people doing\nthat. It's spectacular and crazy. So it wasn't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078612,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people walking in sync that got the bridge\nto wobble. It was the wobbling bridge that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083652,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "got people to walk in sync. And so as the\npeople got in step with the motion of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088532,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bridge, by adopting this weird kind of penguin\ngate, they ended up inadvertently pumping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093732,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more energy into the bridge and making its\nmotion worse. And so this was this positive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098852,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feedback loop between the motion of the crowd,\ncausing the bridge to move more, which causes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103092,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more people to get in step with the bridge,\nwhich made more people you know, drive the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107012,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bridge. Once the problem was identified, they\ncould solve it by decreasing the coupling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112612,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strength. They installed energy dissipating\ndampers all along the bridge. It was a tremendous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118932,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "embarrassment, and it cost several million\npounds to repair the bridge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124292,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In science, we do reductionism, all of our\nscience courses tell us the way to solve a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1128772,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem is to break it into smaller parts "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130612,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and analyze the parts. And \nthat has been phenomenally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133172,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "successful for every branch of science. But\nthe great frontier in science today is what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1138772,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happens when you try to go back to put the\nparts together to understand the whole. That's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1143732,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the field of complex systems. That's why we\ndon't understand the immune system very well."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1149252,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don't understand consciousness very well\nor the economy. It seems like the whole is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153892,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than the sum of the parts. That's the\ncliche that has entranced me for my whole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158292,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "research career. I want to understand how\ncan you figure out the properties of the whole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1163252,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "given the properties of the parts?"
    } ]
  } ]
}
